<ServiceLayout
  serviceName="Buyer Journey + Preferences"
  headline="Why do people buy in your category?"
  subhead="Reconstruct real buying decisions with switch interviews and MaxDiff priority ranking. Your customers appear alongside buyers who chose alternatives, giving you an honest view of why you win, why you lose, and what language drives the market."
  description="KwantumLabs Buyer Analysis uses the Jobs-to-be-Done switch interview framework combined with best-worst MaxDiff scaling to reveal the forces driving purchasing decisions in your category."
  buyingTriggers={[
    "Your positioning was written by your internal team, and you're not sure it reflects what buyers actually say drove their decision",
    "Sales reps are improvising competitive responses instead of working from evidence about how your buyers' priorities differ from buyers who chose alternatives",
    "Product prioritization debates drag on because no one can agree on which outcomes buyers actually ranked first",
    "You need to know the real competitive set in your category, not the one your sales team assumes based on the deals they remember",
    "Your messaging uses your internal language and you suspect buyers describe the problem differently in their own words",
    "You want to understand what triggers buyers to start looking for a solution in the first place, before they ever engage with sales",
  ]}
  deliverables={[
    { name: 'Switching story archetypes', description: '3-5 named buyer journey profiles summarizing the dominant push, pull, anxiety, and habit forces for each segment, with frequency counts showing how common each archetype is.' },
    { name: 'Attribute importance ranking (MaxDiff)', description: 'Forced-rank importance scores for up to 6 outcomes, for buyers who chose your product and buyers who chose an alternative, with statistically defensible separation between top and bottom ranks.' },
    { name: 'Push and pull force frequency tables', description: 'The specific frustrations that drove buyers to start looking, and the specific outcomes that attracted them to a new solution, ranked by frequency with representative verbatim language.' },
    { name: 'Competitive set mention table', description: 'Every alternative buyers named unprompted during interviews, ranked by how often they appeared, showing the real competitive set your buyers are considering.' },
    { name: 'Emergent attribute frequency table', description: 'Outcomes and decision factors buyers raised unprompted that were not part of the MaxDiff design, ranked by frequency, surfacing blind spots in your current positioning and roadmap.' },
    { name: 'Subgroup force comparison', description: 'Side-by-side switching forces for buyers who chose your product vs. buyers who chose an alternative, showing exactly where priorities diverge and what each group was looking for.' },
  ]}
  approach={[
    "Buyer Journey + Preferences is built on the Jobs-to-be-Done switch interview framework, which reconstructs the full decision story from the buyer's perspective. Most win/loss programs ask the sales rep what happened. This study asks the buyer. The difference is significant: what your team thinks drove the decision and what the buyer actually experienced are often not the same thing.",
    "Each 20-minute session blends two methods. The switch interview explores the four forces that drive purchasing decisions: the push that frustrated buyers away from their old solution, the pull that attracted them to a new one, the anxiety that almost stopped them from switching, and the habit that kept them anchored to the status quo. The best-worst MaxDiff component forces buyers to make explicit trade-offs across up to 6 outcomes, producing a rank order grounded in behavior rather than stated preference.",
    "The study is designed around two subgroups: buyers who chose your product and buyers who chose an alternative. The comparison between those two groups is where the strategic insight lives. Where they agree, you are aligned with the market. Where they diverge, you have a positioning decision to make. The minimum sample is 100 participants per subgroup, which produces statistically reliable importance rankings within each group and defensible comparisons between them.",
  ]}
  timeline="Most Buyer Journey + Preferences engagements are completed in 3-4 weeks from kickoff to final presentation, including study design, data collection, analysis, and reporting."
  investment="Typical investment: $35,000-$65,000, depending on the number of attributes tested, subgroups included, and depth of analysis."
  scopeNote="A standard study with two subgroups, 6 MaxDiff attributes, and 100 participants per group sits at the lower end. Studies with additional segments, more attributes, or multiple competitive comparisons sit at the upper end."
  faqs={[
    { question: 'How is this different from a standard win/loss program?', answer: 'Standard win/loss programs ask sales reps to report what they think happened in a deal. This study asks the actual buyer to reconstruct the full decision story using the JTBD switch interview framework. Buyers reveal forces your sales team rarely hears: what frustrated them with their old solution, what almost stopped them from switching, and what habits kept them anchored to the status quo. The buyer perspective and the sales rep perspective are often very different.' },
    { question: 'Who do you interview?', answer: 'Anyone who recently purchased a solution in your category, regardless of which product they chose. Your customers appear alongside buyers who chose competitors. This design is intentional: the comparison between the two groups is where the most valuable insight comes from.' },
    { question: 'What is MaxDiff and why use it instead of a standard ranking survey?', answer: 'Best-worst MaxDiff forces buyers to choose the most and least important item from sets of 4, producing two data points per task. This approach is more reliable than asking people to rate everything on a scale, where most respondents rate everything as "very important." MaxDiff creates genuine discrimination between what buyers truly prioritize and what they consider secondary.' },
    { question: 'How many attributes can we test in the MaxDiff?', answer: 'Up to 6 attributes or outcomes at the standard sample size of 100 participants per subgroup. With 8 sets of 4 attributes and 100 participants, each attribute receives approximately 533 exposures, which clears the reliability threshold for defensible rank-order estimates. Testing more than 6 attributes would require a larger sample.' },
    { question: 'What does the deliverable look like?', answer: 'You receive a strategic report with switching story archetypes, MaxDiff importance rankings for both subgroups, push and pull force frequency tables with verbatim buyer language, the full competitive set ranked by mention frequency, and an emergent attribute frequency table showing blind spots. We present findings live and provide the underlying data.' },
    { question: 'Can this study feed into a conjoint or brand study?', answer: 'Yes. The Buyer Journey + Preferences study produces attribute input sheets that feed directly into both Conjoint: Pricing and Packaging and Brand Associations + CSV studies. Clients who want to go deeper on pricing or brand perception often start here, then design a follow-up study using the attributes and language surfaced by the buying analysis.' },
    { question: 'How long does it take?', answer: 'Most engagements are completed in 3-4 weeks from kickoff to final presentation, including study design, data collection, analysis, and delivery.' },
  ]}
/>
